Day 2 — Network Security, Ports & Packet Capture

What I Learned

Today, I focused on network security fundamentals, network protocols, ports, and packet analysis. I also worked hands-on with my home lab to understand how network traffic moves between different systems.

Network Fundamentals

I learned more about:
	•	What a network is and how devices communicate with each other.
	•	The role of network protocols in controlling how devices communicate.
	•	The difference between end devices and network infrastructure.
	•	How different network services communicate using port numbers.
	•	Why ports can be thought of as entrance doors to services running on a network.

 Understanding Ports

I learned that services listening for network connections are associated with specific port numbers.

For example:
	•	SSH — Port 22
	•	HTTP — Port 80
	•	HTTPS — Port 443
	•	DNS — Port 53

Understanding ports is important for a SOC Analyst because unusual or unexpected connections to ports can provide useful information when investigating network activity.

Promiscuous Mode

I also learned how to configure promiscuous mode in my virtual lab environment.

My lab contains:
	•	Ubuntu Server
	•	Ubuntu Desktop
	•	Windows 10

Promiscuous mode allowed my network interface to receive and inspect packets beyond only the traffic specifically addressed to that interface.

I used this to verify that network packets were flowing across the interfaces in my lab environment.

TShark — Packet Capture

I learned how to use TShark as a command-line packet capture and network analysis tool.

I used TShark to:
	•	Capture network packets.
	•	Monitor traffic moving through my lab.
	•	Observe communication between different machines.
	•	Inspect network protocols and packet information.
	•	Get hands-on experience with network traffic analysis.

Hands-On Lab

I applied what I learned by capturing traffic flowing through my home lab environment and examining the packets generated between my Ubuntu Server, Ubuntu Desktop, and Windows 10 machines.

This helped me connect the theoretical concepts of protocols, ports, interfaces, and packets to real network traffic.

SOC Analyst Connection

Today’s lab helped me understand an important part of SOC analysis:

	Network traffic contains evidence.

By understanding protocols, ports, packet captures, and normal network communication, I can begin identifying unusual traffic that may indicate suspicious activity.

 Key Takeaways
	•	A network allows devices to communicate and exchange data.
	•	Protocols define how network communication takes place.
	•	Ports identify services that are listening for network connections.
	•	Promiscuous mode can be useful for network traffic monitoring in a lab environment.
	•	TShark can capture and analyze packets from the command line.
	•	Packet analysis is an important skill for a SOC Analyst.
	•	Understanding normal network behavior is essential before identifying abnormal or malicious activity.

Day 2 completed 

